<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naganika</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd–1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Naganika was a queen of the Satavahana dynasty, the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satakarni-i">Satakarni I</a>, who ruled the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> probably in the 2nd or 1st century BCE. Her long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> inscription in a cave at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naneghat">Naneghat</a> pass records the Vedic sacrifices performed by the royal family, including horse sacrifices, and the lavish fees paid to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>. It is one of the earliest important records of the dynasty, and coins bearing her name have also been found.</p></div>
